A||N||11||MickeyAsh||Cold Start Valve||||1||
Z||000000||MickeyAsh||11-27-2001||10:36 PM||||Hey guys, I'm going to be buying a cold start valve for my V6 '86 GT soon and I THINK I've found a donor for it, that being an 88 Formula V6... my question is, are the cold start valves the same for those two V6's? (God I hope so, this will save me some cash) Thanks in advance!||||reg||1
Z||000001||MickeyAsh||11-27-2001||10:47 PM||||I suppose I better make sure it's the valve also before buying a new one, lol. Here's what's happenin with my baby. I go out in the morning (BTW I live in FL so right now that's no less than 70 degrees FH) turn the key to on, fuel pump runs, then shuts off, turn key to crank, engine rolls over 4 - 10 seconds, no crank... turn key to off, let fuel pressure die down, key back to on, pump runs, key to crank, 4 - 10 seconds, ALMOST CRANKS and then nothing. Repeat until it finally cranks and purrs like a kitten... it's in fine shape besides this pesky problem. Pretty sure it's some of the cold start stuff, dont know if it's the valve or the switch though, any help will save me some time, hehe. Again, thanks in advance.||||reg||1
Z||000002||watts||11-27-2001||11:33 PM||||Do a search on here for cold start.  I posted the pages right out of the manual for doing the full diag's on it.<BR><P>------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/watts_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||87GTZ34||11-28-2001||12:04 AM||||Mickey,  I also live in Fl a little further south and was thinking that I might disconnect my cold start valve.  I haven't tried it yet though as it is pain to hook back up on the valve itself.  I wouldn't think that in mild weather, we would need it so I'm not sure that is your problem.  If I get a chance this week, I will try my test and let you know.||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||11-28-2001||09:25 AM||||If you want to disable the cold start valve, might be easiest to just pull the connector off the cold start switch.  Cold start switch is screwed into top RHS of intake manifold, it's a big hexagonal thing.  Compress the little wire "croquet wicket" on the connector to release it.||||reg||3
Z||000005||FTF Engineering||11-28-2001||09:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87GTZ34:<BR><B>Mickey,  I also live in Fl a little further south and was thinking that I might disconnect my cold start valve.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by StuGood:<BR><B>If you want to disable the cold start valve, might be easiest to just pull the connector off the cold start switch</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not a problem.<P>I disabled my cold start valve about two years ago and can barely tell any difference.<P>I live in PA... If it doesn't cause me a problem, you can be pretty durn sure that it wouldn't cause you any problems in FL!!<P>I did it as StuGood described by disconnecting the cold start switch.<P>Try it. It's easy to do, and if you don't like the results and change your mind, you can simply reconnect the cold start switch connector.<P>-Bruce at FTF Engineering||||reg||1
Z||000006||Neal||11-28-2001||10:35 PM||||Problem with disconecting the wireing harness is if your problems are a leaky cold start injector, even disabled it will still leak.  youll have to remove it entirly.  But yes ANY v6 fiero cold start injector is the same||||reg||1
Z||000007||ka4nkf||11-29-2001||12:09 AM||||I would also check out the ECM coolant sensor switch. This tells the computer If the engine is hot or cold.||||reg||1
Z||000008||FTF Engineering||11-29-2001||08:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Neal:<BR><B>Problem with disconecting the wireing harness is if your problems are a leaky cold start injector, even disabled it will still leak.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hehe... Yup. Good point. Can't fix that electronically. Have to resort to plumbing for that one!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ka4nkf:<BR><B>I would also check out the ECM coolant sensor switch. This tells the computer If the engine is hot or cold.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The ECM coolant switch doesn't impact the cold start valve. The only things that affect the cold start valve are the cold start switch (thermotime switch) and what position the key is in.<P>ECM coolant sensor has nothing to do with the cold start valve.<P>-Bruce at FTF Engineering||||reg||1
Z||000009||ka4nkf||11-29-2001||08:54 PM||||FTF Engineering<BR>The ECM temp. sensor tells the ECM if the engine is hot or cold and the ECM will control the Fuel flow according to the temp.<BR>also the cold start switch is in line with the starter switch and only works when starter is engaged. there is another sensor that controls the fuel flow and that sensor is in the air cleaner housing. it is called a MAT sensor.(manifold air temp.<P>These two sensors are tricky to understand but they work.<BR>I put a 10,000 ohm resister in the MAT sensor and it makes the ECM think the air is cold and it gives it more fuel makes it start up real good. also no noticeable gas mileage drop. also note that when the engine gets hot the MAT sensor is no longer in use. the ECM sensor takes over.<P>ok that's enough from me.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||MickeyAsh||11-30-2001||12:24 AM||||Well I just gave her 4 coats of wax, maybe it'll startup better now... heheheheh... anyway, my cold start valve is on the way whether that's the problem or not. 11 bucks on ebay, I know it's used and all but hey, better than a failed one... another question here though, does the cold start valve affect the engine's cranking time when the air temp outside is 70*? It's my understanding that by "cold start" the sensor pours more fuel in to cause a stronger combustion and an easier crank... if the cold start valve isn't even in use in 70* temp, what could be causing my cranking issues? Mind you, it only gives me trouble when it's been sitting for 3+ hours... if I crank it, go run some errands, turn it off and come back soon, it fires RIGHT up, I mean, as soon as the starter engages, it's purring... I really wanna get this fixed...||||reg||1
Z||000011||87GTZ34||12-05-2001||12:15 PM||||Stu,<P>  I looked at the switch end too but it is very cluttered there as well.  I did pull it on the switch end to see how it affect the starting.  <P>  Upper 70s and it starts harder both cold and hot.  My engine is in good shape had the heads done and a tune-up 1500 miles ago.  With the codl start hooked up, my engine will start in 1 or 2 turns of the starter.  With out it it is 5 to 7 so I will be hooking it back up (dread that one).  <P>  I think even if the car is in moderate temps (like the mid atlantic states), the cold start system will make be necessary.  I think the design of the intake idle air circuit/injector placement dictates the need for it.  Gm redesigned other 2.8s to save cost by removing the cold start.||||reg||3
